In polar coordinates the acceleration is
$
  bold(dot.double(x)) = (dot.double(r) - r dot(theta)^2) bold(hat(r)) + (r dot.double(theta) + 2 dot(r) dot(theta)) bold(hat(theta)).
$
With $r$ constant, so that $dot(r) = dot.double(r) = 0$, and constant angular speed $omega = dot(theta)$ with $dot.double(theta) = 0$, only the radial term survives:
$
  bold(dot.double(x)) = - r omega^2 bold(hat(r)).
$
The acceleration points towards the origin, and by Newton's second law a force of magnitude $m r omega^2$ towards the origin is required.
